    "text": "Finding: Muscle activity (EMG) in 22 infants: highest erector spinae (back) activity when prone; lowest cervical paraspinal (neck) activity in the car seat. Caregivers reported 12% of awake time prone, 43% in supine-lying baby gear.\nSources: Siddicky et al. (2020), Positioning and baby devices impact infant spinal muscle activity, Journal of Biomechanics, DOI 10.1016/j.jbiomech.2020.109741.\nConfidence: MODERATE. Small lab study; describes muscle use, not outcomes.\n\nFinding: Positional plagiocephaly in 7- to 12-week-olds in Calgary: 46.6% (205 of 440), 78.3% mild.\nSources: Mawji et al. (2013), The incidence of positional plagiocephaly: a cohort study, Pediatrics, DOI 10.1542/peds.2012-3438.\nConfidence: HIGH for this sample; other studies report lower figures (e.g., 37.8% at 8 to 12 weeks, Ballardini 2018), depending on the tool."
